6. Dashboard Visualization:
Using Wazuh, a dashboard was configured to visualize alert severity levels.
The dashboard displayed:
Distribution of alerts (Critical, High, medium, and Low)
Top attack sources
Most triggered detection rules
Alert trends over time
This helps SOC analysts quickly identify high-priority threats.
7. Incident Ticket Creation
When a critical alert is detected, an incident ticket is created in TheHive.
Example Incident Ticket
Title:
[Critical] Ransomware Detected on Server-X
Description:
Suspicious ransomware activity detected on Server-X. The file crypto_locker.exe was executed, and unusual outbound communication was detected from IP address 192.168.1.50.
Indicators of Compromise (IOCs):
Malicious File: crypto_locker.exe
Suspicious IP: 192.168.1.50
Modified encrypted files detected.
8. Escalation Procedure
Critical alerts must be escalated to Tier-2 analysts for deeper investigation.
Escalation Steps
Verify the alert and confirm indicators.
Collect evidence such as logs and affected host details.
Contain the threat (e.g., isolate the system).
Create an incident ticket in the Hive.
Escalate the incident to Tier-2 SOC analysts.
Example escalation communication includes:
Summary of incident.
Indicators of compromise.
Actions taken during initial triage.
Required next steps.
9. Outcome
Through this exercise, the following skills were developed:
Alert triage and prioritization.
Incident documentation.
Threat mapping using MITRE ATT&CK.
Security monitoring using Wazuh dashboards.
Incident management using Hive.
